Before MAGA: How 19th-Century Newspapers Gave Voice to America’s First Nativist Movement
A computational journalism project by Natalie Holly Purviance

Overview:

This project analyzes nearly a decade of newspaper coverage surrounding the Know Nothing movement—America’s first major nativist, anti-immigrant political coalition active between 1845–1855. Using Python, web scraping, text analysis, and natural language processing (NLP), the project uncovers rhetorical patterns, recurring themes, and geographic circulation of nativist messaging across early American newspapers.
The analysis draws on a dataset originally scraped from the Library of Congress Chronicling America historical newspaper archive. Articles were collected from New York, Massachusetts, and Maryland papers across the pre-Civil War period.

Why This Project:

The Know Nothings provide a crucial historical link between 19th-century xenophobia and today’s political discourse. Their rhetoric—fear of foreign influence, suspicion of immigrants, conspiracy-laden warnings of national decline—echoes contemporary right-wing narratives and the broader resurgence of nativist politics.

This project seeks to:

Examine how early newspapers constructed racialized and religious “threats”
Map the circulation of nativist rhetoric across states
Compare 19th-century persuasion tactics to modern misinformation patterns
Demonstrate the power of computational methods in historical research

Methodology
1. Data Collection
Articles were scraped from the Library of Congress Chronicling America API
Filters: 1845–1855, keyword “Know Nothing,” three states (NY, MA, MD)
Approximately 3,000 articles were retrieved at the time of scraping

Important Note:
During the course of this project, the Library of Congress changed their API.
This meant the query structure and access endpoints no longer matched the version used during initial data collection. Fortunately, the dataset had already been scraped and saved locally. I uploaded that saved dataset directly into the notebook so I could continue the NLP and analysis workflow without disruption.

2. Data Cleaning
Text normalization
Removal of OCR errors
Filtering out non-editorial content
Deduplication of similar articles

3. Natural Language Processing (NLP)
Techniques used:
Tokenization and stopword removal
Keyword extraction
Topic clustering
Frequency analysis of rhetorical frames
Named entity recognition (NER) for identifying targeted groups
Sentiment characterization of nativist language
